00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:34.230
So since Saudi Arabia recently started things
that we're doing more progressive, not where

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:38.590
we would want them to be, obviously, but,
you know, they're allowing women to drive.

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:46.200
Do you think that if we never went into Afghanistan
or if we left maybe 10 years ago, that they

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:53.140
would naturally progress towards progressiveness
as we see a lot of these more conservative

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.780
countries tend to do over the years?

00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:02.620
And additionally, what major legislation would
you do with that money if we never went into

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:05.579
Afghanistan or we left after maybe 10 years?

00:01:05.579 --> 00:01:11.179
Speaker 1: Well, the second part is, is, you
know, there's no specific legislation.

00:01:11.179 --> 00:01:15.359
It's all the things we've been talking about
already that they love to say, well, how are

00:01:15.359 --> 00:01:16.429
we going to pay for that?

00:01:16.429 --> 00:01:17.529
They'd be saying anyway.

00:01:17.529 --> 00:01:21.959
But, you know, we're talking about expanding
health care, improving education, advancing

00:01:21.959 --> 00:01:23.849
alternative and renewable energy.

00:01:23.849 --> 00:01:24.990
The list would be the same.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:31.599
It's just we would have one more, one more
sort of sort of like a tranche of money available.

00:01:31.599 --> 00:01:36.119
But they would still say we don't have the
money to pay for it in terms of whether Afghanistan

00:01:36.119 --> 00:01:43.009
would have sort of naturally on its own liberalized
had we never gone in or left 10 years ago.

00:01:43.009 --> 00:01:44.090
It's an impossible question.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:48.119
I think it's completely impossible to say
there are some countries where that has been

00:01:48.119 --> 00:01:50.080
the direction and others where it hasn't.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:53.429
But I think what's important to know about
Saudi Arabia and I recently read a good book

00:01:53.429 --> 00:01:59.710
about it, about the rise of Mohammed bin Salman,
is that there is a big component of it that's

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:05.469
basically marketing and optics where it's
in order to be able to get Saudi Arabia wants

00:02:05.469 --> 00:02:10.759
to diversify away from oil and they want to
take the oil money and invest elsewhere, like

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:15.540
in Hollywood films and in dotcom startups,
tech startups, et cetera.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:22.770
And in order to be better seen in that world,
they've done some of the liberalization.

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:28.360
And so I'm not saying it's not in good faith
or that it is, but I think there's a big aspect

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:33.290
of this that's to their advantage for their
financial goals.

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:38.420
And so I have no idea in Afghanistan, it's
a very different situation, but I don't think

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:43.060
we would be any worse off had we left 10,
12, 14, 16, 18 years ago.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:44.450
Speaker 5: Mm hmm.

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:45.450
Yeah.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:50.090
I mean, I'd have preferred to see us leave,
uh, maybe never have gone in, but I don't

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:51.090
know.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:53.409
I was too young to really understand what
was going on in that time.

00:02:53.409 --> 00:02:55.330
